🔹 1. On the client machine (where you connect from)

Make sure you have an X server installed:

On Linux: usually already there (xorg-x11-xauth, xorg-x11-x11-utils).

On Windows: install Xming, VcXsrv, or MobaXterm.

On macOS: install XQuartz.

When connecting with SSH, use:
ssh -X username@remote_host

or for trusted forwarding:
ssh -Y username@remote_host

🔹 2. On the remote server (where you connect to)

Install X11 utilities if not already present:

sudo apt-get install xauth x11-apps    # Debian/Ubuntu
sudo yum install xorg-x11-xauth xorg-x11-apps  # RHEL/CentOS


Edit the SSH daemon config:
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config

Make sure these lines exist (uncomment or add if missing):

X11Forwarding yes
X11DisplayOffset 10
X11UseLocalhost yes


Save and restart SSH:

sudo systemctl restart sshd   # RHEL/CentOS/Ubuntu 16.04+
sudo service ssh restart      # Older systems

🔹 3. Test it

After reconnecting with ssh -X or ssh -Y, try:
xclock

or
xeyes

If an X window pops up on your local machine, forwarding is working ✅.

⚠️ Common Issues

If you get Error: Can't open display, check:

On client: echo $DISPLAY should show something like localhost:10.0.

On server: xauth list should list keys.

Your firewall/SELinux might block X11 (check /var/log/secure or /var/log/auth.log).
